WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:15.920
This is a production of Cornell University

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:27.120
The Long Island Horticultural Research and&nbsp;
Extension Center is a 68-acre Cornell University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.440
facility on the eastern end of the island.&nbsp;The mission of our research center is to help&nbsp;&nbsp;our agricultural community out here

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:40.400
 in Suffolk&nbsp;County succeed. The main focus is applied research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.160
and education and I think that is something&nbsp;
that really the growers can't get elsewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.960
we focus on Long Island soils and microclimates&nbsp;
and being in this maritime climate we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.720
pretty unique compared to the rest of the state.&nbsp;And that's important to have that research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.600
and education that is directly applicable to our&nbsp;
area. We are an extension of Cornell University. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:12.400
are providing you with the information that's done&nbsp;
at Cornell University and also the very applicable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.120
research that's done here at the research lab&nbsp;
and we're extending that out into the community.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.600
I think it's important to have this resource&nbsp;
for growers and landscape professionals for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.480
a number of reasons. Probably the&nbsp;
most important is that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.280
day-to-day problems and issues that need to&nbsp;
be solved that can put them out of business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.880
If a grower is not sustainable, they're not&nbsp;
going to stay in production. So sustainable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.440
how they're growing things sustainable in their&nbsp;
business are all very important. So I'm looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.200
managing plant diseases in the best possible way&nbsp;
-- the most effective way so they're going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.600
minimal costs going into control because&nbsp;
they're using the best possible practices.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.480
Implementing sustainable practices, all farmers&nbsp;
want to increase the sustainability of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.480
operation. All farmers are looking to improve soil&nbsp;
health and have a better crop. At the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:21.680
day, farms are businesses and sustainability&nbsp;
and longevity is extremely important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:29.760
We've changed over time from a philosophy of using&nbsp;
a chemical to stop a problem to using more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.640
strategy to stop a problem. We have a program with&nbsp;
orchards where we've introduced mating disruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.040
for example. So that can substitute for the use&nbsp;
of pesticides. It also can give you better control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.520
than you would have with with insecticides.&nbsp;
So that's widely been adopted here. It's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:55.040
successful. It works quite well. Inside this&nbsp;
tube, there is the same chemicals that female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.120
insects actually release. This pheromone mating&nbsp;
disruption technology is a non-insecticidal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:08.160
control method and it is organically compatible.&nbsp;
So this is good for the environment. So you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.200
have to use any insecticides when you are&nbsp;
using the insect mating disruption ties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:23.520
It's important to me that growers understand when&nbsp;
it's too late to make a pesticide application or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.404
when it's important to do something at a certain&nbsp;
time in relation to the crop production cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.404 --> 00:03:35.124
because then they're not wasting chemicals&nbsp;
that could otherwise just not be put into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:40.324
environment. If they're doing the right thing at&nbsp;
the right time then they achieve their objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.324 --> 00:03:48.084
and they're not polluting. Soil health 
is critical. Soil is a living system and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.084 --> 00:03:52.896
how we manage it has a direct impact --&nbsp;
not just on farms and in the crops being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.016 --> 00:03:58.376
grown -- but environmentally downstream. And&nbsp;
with the agriculture stewardship program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.728
we're focusing on protecting our ground&nbsp;
and surface waters here on Long Island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.840
We're trying to maintain the aquifer which&nbsp;
is the sole source of our drinking water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.964 --> 00:04:14.924
as pure as it can be. We need to figure out a way&nbsp;
to manage the weeds even without some of the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.128 --> 00:04:19.768
that other people have. When not only you see your&nbsp;
research and are able to show growers the research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:27.840
is successful, but they adopt it, that's when you&nbsp;
know you've been you've really made a difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:35.280
Having a place where we have specialists&nbsp;
from entomologists to plant pathologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.240
to soil scientists and floriculturalists is&nbsp;
so critical. Agriculture is a system and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.399
all need to collaborate and work together&nbsp;
to be the best resource for our farmers.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:16.000
This has been a production of Cornell&nbsp;
University, on the web at cornell.edu.

